      "content": "In contemporary secular societies, arguments that align with modern concepts of individual rights and autonomy often resonate more strongly, mainly because they appeal to shared societal values of personal freedom and self-determination. Liberals' arguments, by emphasizing dignity and suffering, tap into these values and thus could be perceived as more compelling in societies that prioritize human rights.\n\nConversely, the conservative defense of abstract principles may be seen as less directly relevant to individual experiences, despite their moral significance. This doesn't mean that their arguments are without strength—conservatives advocate for unwavering respect for life, which holds serious moral weight—rather, their objections may not have the same practical impact in a society that deals primarily in terms of individual rights and freedoms.\n\nReflecting on this in more secular terms, the liberal perspective could potentially be seen as more congruent with the autonomy central to modern secular thought, while the conservative position, though robust, may seem tied more to traditional religious values, which some may argue exist alongside rather than at the center of secular philosophy.",
